In programming (especially in Java),
User Defined Data Types (UDTs) are data types
that you create yourself
 using built-in types like int, float, or String.

They help you model real-world entities (like Student, Employee, Car, etc.) in your program.

🔹 What is a User Defined Data Type?
A User Defined Data Type is a data type created by the programmer using:
class
interface
enum
array (sometimes considered)

👉 Most commonly: class is used

🔹 Why use UDT?
Because real-world data is complex.
Example:
A student has:
name
age
marks

👉 You cannot store all this in a single int or String, so you create a custom type (class).